  "text": "Samples containing hyaluronic acid were obtained by filtering liquid from cow joints and from a protein‑rich fluid taken from a human cancer case. The cow material was stripped of protein using organic solvents and then separated on an ion‑exchange resin, yielding several fractions. All samples remained stable when kept frozen at –20 °C. The cow preparation still held about one‑fifth protein, while the other samples had less than six percent. Sedimentation and flow‑behavior tests showed the two sources behaved almost identically, and treating the cow sample with a specific enzyme did not alter its viscosity. Light‑scattering and viscosity data suggested the molecules are highly extended but not simple random coils, possibly featuring some branching or cross‑links that add rigidity. After ion‑exchange, the average molecular weight stayed the same, though the size of the molecules increased. Lowering the pH to about 3 caused the structures to contract, with only a small re‑expansion when the pH returned to neutral. Optical rotation measurements supported a more complex shape than a linear coil. Osmotic pressure experiments with mixed protein and hyaluronic acid solutions gave results similar to earlier reports, but differed from some partition data. Refractive‑index measurements paired with light‑scattering provided a convenient way to determine hyaluronic‑acid concentrations."
